<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Piombino</h1><p class="st-meta">1399 CE – 1805 CE · capital Piombino</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Tuscan coastal state with the iron mines of Elba, ruled by the Appiani and later the Ludovisi and Boncompagni.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1399 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gherardo-appiani">Gherardo Appiani</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, sold the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> but kept Piombino, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> and nearby islands as his own lordship. His family ruled it as lords and, from 1594, as imperial princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Piombino controlled the iron mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> and the channel between Elba and the mainland, a strategic route for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> shipping. Its small size made it dependent on stronger powers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portoferraio">Portoferraio</a> on Elba in 1548, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> later held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-longone">Porto Longone</a>. After the Appiani died out, the principality passed to the Ludovisi in 1634 and then to the Boncompagni-Ludovisi.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> gave Piombino to his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa</a>, merging it into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-lucca-and-piombino">Principality of Lucca and Piombino</a>. In 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-piombino.jpg" alt="Principality of Piombino" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: User Hypergio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandiera_Appiani.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1399 CE – 1805 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Piombino</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gherardo Appiani, Niccolò Ludovisi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tuscan Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lordship of Piombino, Piombino</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1602&amp;focus=principality-of-piombino" aria-label="Principality of Piombino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4934 653 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Piombino lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5254 909L5250 884M5254 909L4910 989M5254 909L5252 880"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5250 884h0M4910 989h0M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5254 909h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5254 909h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Piombino. <a href="/atlas?year=1602&amp;focus=principality-of-piombino">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
